      "latency_ms": 2077.5,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on Earth.** It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through different lenses:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nChemically, water is represented by the formula **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. \n* This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* Water is a \"polar\" molecule. This means the oxygen side has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge. Because of this, water molecules act like microscopic magnets, clinging to each other and to other substances. This gives water unique properties, such as the ability to dissolve many substances (making it the \"universal solvent\").\n\n### 2. Physical States\nWater is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states of matter:\n* **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, swim in, and find in rivers and oceans.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). Uniquely, solid water is less dense than liquid water, which is why ice floats.\n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water evaporates or boils at 100°C (212°F).\n\n### 3. The Ecological & Planetary View\nWater defines our planet. \n* **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly moving in a continuous cycle—evaporating from the oceans into the atmosphere, condensing into clouds, falling back to Earth as rain or snow (precipitation), and flowing back to the oceans via rivers.\n\n### 4. The Biological View (Life)\nWater is the medium of life. \n* The human body is made up of about **60% water**, and the brain and heart are roughly 73% water.\n* Water acts as a solvent, transporting nutrients, regulating body temperature, flushing out waste, and providing the moist environment required for cellular chemical reactions to take place. Without water, biochemical processes simply cannot happen.\n\n"
